Overloading vs Overriding in Java

In Java, both overloading and overriding are important concepts related to method definitions. They are often confused with each other, but they serve different purposes and have distinct characteristics.

Overloading

Overloading is the process of defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters. The methods can have different data types, number of parameters, or order of parameters. The method that is called depends on the arguments passed to it.

Example:

public class OverloadingExample {
public void print(int num) {
System.out.println("Integer: " + num);
}
public void print(double num) {
System.out.println("Double: " + num);
}
public void print(String str) {
System.out.println("String: " + str);
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
OverloadingExample obj = new OverloadingExample();
obj.print(10); // Calls print(int num)
obj.print(10.5); // Calls print(double num)
obj.print("Hello"); // Calls print(String str)
}

